{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>See, YHWH your Elohim has set before you the land; go up, take possession.</b> It should have said, 'Behold, YHWH your Elohim has given you the land'; but it alludes to their prince (שר), for since He has given their prince before them on high, surely the land below will be theirs. And this is what it says: עלה רש — 'go up, take possession' — that is, go up against the prince (שר); for inasmuch as He is YHWH your Elohim, the Most High overpowers the prince and sets the land before you. Therefore it says ראה ('see'), which is a term of contemplation and vision of the heart." "<b>And from this</b> they said in the Midrash: ראה נתן ה' אלהיך לפניך — 'See, YHWH your Elohim has set before you' — was it literally before them? Rather, He felled its prince first. And so He mentioned above: ראה נתתי לפניכם את הארץ — 'See, I have given before you the land' — and then returned and said: בואו ורשו את הארץ — 'Come and possess the land' — and it should have said ורשו אותה, 'possess it.' But the meaning is: See, I have given before you on high the prince of the land, and from now on, come and possess the land, for there is no one to contest the matter. And so He will mention further, concerning the blessed Name (Deuteronomy 2:31): ראה החלותי תת לפניך את סיחון ואת ארצו החל רש לרשת את ארצו — 'See, I have begun to deliver before you Sihon and his land; begin to possess his land.' That is: I have begun to deliver before you on high Sihon and his land, and from now on, do your part — begin to possess his land below. And what it says by way of החלותי, 'I have begun,' should have been said in the language of completion, for since the Holy One, blessed be He, has begun this, it is upon you to complete the matter; but it says החל רש to indicate that as soon as Israel begins, the matter will be completed and they will possess his land, since the Holy One, blessed be He, has begun with what is the essence. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded regarding Nebuchadnezzar: at the time the Holy One, blessed be He, took vengeance upon him, He took vengeance upon his prince first, as it is said (Daniel 4:28): עוד מלתא בפום מלכא קל מן שמיא נפל — 'While the word was in the king's mouth, a voice (קל) fell from heaven.' What is קל? Rabbi Yehoshua said: the prince of Nebuchadnezzar was called קל, and the Holy One, blessed be He, felled him from heaven, as it says: קל מן שמיא נפל — 'a voice fell from heaven' — and afterward He took vengeance upon Nebuchadnezzar himself, as it says (ibid.): לך אמרין נבוכדנצר מלכותא עדת מינך — 'To you it is said, O Nebuchadnezzar: the kingdom has departed from you.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:51.805703787Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}